      en: "A book whose argument is so simple in retrospect that it seems strange no one wrote it earlier: the symptoms of disease — fever, pain, nausea, anxiety — are often not malfunctions but adaptations, designed by natural selection to do something useful. Fever is a defense. Coughing is a clearance. Morning sickness may protect a fetus from toxins. Anxiety may be a calibrated alarm. From this single move grows a whole research program, now known as evolutionary or Darwinian medicine. Nesse, a psychiatrist, and Williams, one of the major evolutionary biologists of the twentieth century, write together with the patient clarity that characterizes the best popular science. The book has aged well — many of its specific claims have been argued over and refined; the framework has, if anything, become more central.",
      zh: "一本其论点回看之时简单到「奇怪为何没人更早写出来」的书：疾病的症状——发烧、疼痛、恶心、焦虑——常常并非「故障」，而是「适应」，被自然选择设计来去做某种有用之事。发烧是一种防御。咳嗽是一种清扫。晨吐或可保护胎儿避开毒素。焦虑或是一只被校准过的警报。从这一单一动作展开的，是如今被称为进化医学或达尔文医学的整套研究纲领。尼斯（一位精神科医生）与威廉斯（二十世纪进化生物学的重要人物之一）共同执笔，带着标志着最佳科普之作的那种耐心清晰。本书没有过时——其中许多具体论断都被争论与精化过；而它的框架，毋宁说，已变得更为中心。",
